window.FY_BODY={"id":"2025-11-01_sacramentos-absent","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"1.0 On the alluvial plain of the arid Central Valley, the Sacramento and American rivers collide, merge, and flow south, unfurling into a long, winding Y. California’s capital spreads along the eastern…","excerptZh":"1.0 在干燥的中央谷地的冲积平原上，萨克拉门托河和美国河相撞后合流南下，舒展成一个迤逦的Y字，加州首府就在这河流的东岸铺展开来。 可能是某个城市规划者…","htmlEn":"<h2 class=\"lm lm-sec\" id=\"en-lm1\"><span class=\"lm-no\">1.0</span></h2><p>On the alluvial plain of the arid Central Valley, the Sacramento and American rivers collide, merge, and flow south, unfurling into a long, winding Y. California’s capital spreads along the eastern bank.<br><br>Perhaps some city planner suffered from biophobia. Interstate 5 runs north to south, hugging the river like a levee and earnestly advising the city’s residents to keep a respectful distance from the water. In any event, looking west from downtown Sacramento, an eight-lane artificial riverbed has replaced what should have been a beautiful waterfront. US 50, running east to west, is a length of rebar driven through the city’s spinal cord, brutally splitting it into northern and southern halves. The two freeways meet at the city’s navel, forming one enormous X-shaped bandage.<br><br>Why two strips? When the wound runs this deep, one bandage will not cover it.</p><h2 class=\"lm lm-sec\" id=\"en-lm2\"><span class=\"lm-no\">2.0</span></h2><p>Walking through downtown Sacramento, I felt I had gone back twenty years, to the era when Beijing was bidding for the Olympics and middle-school politics textbooks summed up the world as “one superpower and several major powers.” At the time, that one superpower stood unrivaled in its glory.<br><br>Sacramento presents a peculiar kind of modernity: broad roads, orderly blocks, towers everywhere, easy transportation. In the sunlight, its all-glass facades make an eloquent argument. But these towers were all built twenty years ago. Seen from the 2020s, they are gleaming and unmistakably out of date.<br><br>The so-called “agency towers” around the California State Capitol are almost all products of public-sector-led investment that began in the 1990s.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#en-ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\" data-n=\"1\" data-ref=\"en-ref-1\"><span class=\"num\">1</span><span><a tabindex=\"-1\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_tallest_buildings_in_Sacramento\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">List of tallest buildings in Sacramento</a>, Wikipedia, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></span> Back then, people were full of confidence. They thought the city’s public sector would keep swelling forever—until the 2008 subprime mortgage crisis sent some ambitious types into retreat, freezing the skyline in the shape it has today.<br><br>Walking through downtown Sacramento, I felt I was in America’s Dongguan.<br><br>Two years earlier, on the evening of May 20—5/20, “I love you”—I had seen two skyscrapers filling the view in downtown Dongguan display slogans so absurd they left me speechless: “Warmly Celebrate World Metrology Day” and “Advertising Space Available.” At the time, I thought Dongguan had nothing but a presentable shell. Economic transition and industrial upgrading were a volcanic eruption; their ash froze Dongguan like Pompeii.<br><br>Sacramento’s volcanic ash, meanwhile, drifted down from two dark clouds: the contraction of the public sector and the exhaustion of its political narrative. After the financial crisis, the plans, projections, visions, and blueprints on which the city had lived disappeared. So did Sacramento’s spirit.<br><br>Walking through downtown Sacramento, with hardly another pedestrian in sight, I felt I was in California’s Xiong’an New Area. But I have never been to Xiong’an, so I hope I am wrong.</p><h2 class=\"lm lm-sec\" id=\"en-lm3\"><span class=\"lm-no\">3.0</span></h2><p>Forty miles outside the city, on the upper reaches of the American River, lies the place where gold was first discovered in California. Sacramento was therefore the starting point of the California Gold Rush. Adventurers came upriver from San Francisco and gathered here. The city grew into an inland port, and its teeming old district lingered on along the riverbank.<br><br>I used two phrases: <em>old district</em> and <em>lingered</em>. Neither is respectable language. Official documents from the 1950s used a different description: “one of the worst slum areas in the United States is on our own front doorstep”—a stain stuck to the city’s face, just steps from the Capitol.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#en-ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\" data-n=\"2\" data-ref=\"en-ref-2\"><span class=\"num\">2</span><span><a tabindex=\"-1\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UEUNt6_oYtI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PBS Documentary: REPLACING THE PAST - Sacramento’s Redevelopment History</a>, YouTube, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></span><br><br>America was then in its golden age: the treasury was full, and the people looked eagerly upward. A small handful of overfed young people might have been making deafening rock music or preaching nihilism, but mainstream society paid them no mind. Progress, order, development—those were the serious business. So we all know what happened next.<br><br>Postwar Sacramento welcomed a grand redevelopment plan that would give it a new face.<br><br>Whether by design or not, the project included a north–south freeway built along the river, ideally placed to clear the old district efficiently off the waterfront. (Had the meddlesome President Kennedy not intervened, Interstate 5 would not have needed to make a special bend, preserving the tiny riverside historic quarter that sits there today like a specimen.) Most residents of the old district were ethnic minorities—Chinese, Japanese, Mexican, and Black—and modestly paid members of the working class. Yes, of course they opposed the plan. But theirs was not, after all, the mainstream view. So they protested, to no great consequence, and then scattered to the four winds. In an old documentary, countless men and women in brimmed hats appeared at a ceremony atop the ruins on the day ground was broken for the first federal building. Shovels in hand, they looked very much like a crowd of respectable working people.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#en-ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><br><br>We have encountered a concept like this before: the whole-nation system. To some, immense top-down plans interfere too much with the market, distort organic demand, and are prone to stall, break loose, or seal themselves inside dangerous echo chambers.<br><br>In another context, there is a more refined phrase: public-sector-led investment. Which is to say, the state and capital make the money; vulnerable communities pay the price. Sacramento was the pioneer and paragon of this formulation. Its Redevelopment Agency created Tax Increment Financing (TIF)—using future increases in tax revenue as collateral and a source of repayment—a system that would later sweep across the United States and draw vast sums of investment. The redevelopment project also received the 1963 Ward Melville Gold Medal.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#en-ref-3\" aria-label=\"Reference 3\">3</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\" data-n=\"3\" data-ref=\"en-ref-3\"><span class=\"num\">3</span><span><a tabindex=\"-1\" href=\"https://themetropole.blog/2018/04/12/sacramento-city-of-redevelopments/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SACRAMENTO: CITY OF REDEVELOPMENTS</a>, The Metropole, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></span><br><br>Beneath Capitol Mall, where land values rose seventy-fivefold, Sacramento severed its own historical arteries and buried its once-diverse old district. This was not a bad idea. Some things do not bear close inspection. Everyone is happier when they disappear into dust and smoke.</p><h2 class=\"lm lm-sec\" id=\"en-lm4\"><span class=\"lm-no\">4.0</span></h2><p>When I walked into the Sacramento History Museum, the woman selling tickets at the counter asked me out of nowhere, “You came because you know we have more YouTube followers than any museum in the world, right?” I froze.<br><br>No, ma’am. We were just walking around the historic district. But since we were already at the door, we thought we might as well wander in.<br><br>I later looked at the museum’s account. Its most popular video was a short clip of an old man operating a historic printing press to make postcards. According to the woman, a staff member had started posting bits of daily museum life for fun during the pandemic, and then the account took off. She probably had no idea just how freakishly unpredictable virality is. These days, anything has a shot at five minutes of fame.<br><br>I say this because the museum is nowhere near as fascinating as the words <em>number one in the world</em> make it sound. It has no exhibition it can really show off. The two-story building divides its displays among three subjects: the transformation of the Campbell Soup cannery, the roots of Sacramento’s Mexican culture, and the Native peoples who once lived on this plain. At a brisk pace, half an hour is plenty.<br><br>The real history is outside the museum. Two hundred years ago, this was the terminus of the First Transcontinental Railroad. Chinese laborers made up 90 percent of the workers who built its 1,100-kilometer western portion; more than a thousand died building it.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#en-ref-4\" aria-label=\"Reference 4\">4</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\" data-n=\"4\" data-ref=\"en-ref-4\"><span class=\"num\">4</span><span><a tabindex=\"-1\" href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/zh-hans/%E7%AC%AC%E4%B8%80%E6%A2%9D%E6%A9%AB%E8%B2%AB%E5%A4%A7%E9%99%B8%E9%90%B5%E8%B7%AF#%E6%8B%9B%E8%81%98%E5%B7%A5%E4%BA%BA%E7%9A%84%E5%9B%B0%E5%A2%83\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">First Transcontinental Railroad</a>, Wikipedia, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></span> Directly beneath the embankment where the line finally ended lay Sacramento’s buried Chinatown.<br><br>Of all this, you will not find a single word inside the history museum with more followers than any other museum in the world.</p><h2 class=\"lm lm-sec\" id=\"en-lm5\"><span class=\"lm-no\">5.0</span></h2><p>Two hundred years ago, Chinese adventurers landed in San Francisco, came to Sacramento to rest, and then went on to try their luck in the mines. These immigrants called San Francisco <em>Dai Fow</em>, the Big Port, and Sacramento <em>Yee Fow</em>, the Second Port. At its peak, 5,000 Chinese people lived here.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#en-ref-5\" aria-label=\"Reference 5\">5</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\" data-n=\"5\" data-ref=\"en-ref-5\"><span class=\"num\">5</span><span><a tabindex=\"-1\" href=\"http://www.yeefow.com/past/1850.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">An Evidentiary Timeline on the History of Sacramento’s Chinatown</a>, Yee Fow, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></span><br><br>Today, downtown Sacramento still has something nominally called “Chinatown”: the Sacramento Chinatown Mall, itself a product of the 1960s redevelopment plan. Coming down from the elevated road along the river and heading into the city, you cannot miss it. Seven or eight buildings stand loosely scattered about, each wearing a Chinese-style roofline that is jarringly out of place. Walk in and you find a paifang inscribed “沙加缅度华埠”—Sacramento Chinatown. Its vermilion paint has faded somewhat, yet the gate still looks a little too new.<br><br>Farther in, you find something Thai called Lotus. Aside from the senior apartment building next door, it is the only place open. All the other buildings are locked tight, though they look like public facilities of some kind—clan halls, perhaps, or ancestral shrines. The whole district is empty of people, its doorways empty of footsteps.<br><br>Its emptiness does not make me lonely. A Chinatown artificially inseminated by Western taste and capital, it is only a rootless idol, a monument made entirely of form. It represents nothing, least of all the lives, memories, neighbors, and communities that were destroyed.<br><br>Its builders want to show you a harmless slice of history, one that displays the care and compensation those in power have extended to the Chinese community. In this slice, Chinese culture is vermilion lacquer, roof eaves, a paifang. Shadows move across it as if many people had passed through, but you cannot stop a single one. No one answers your mourning or your questions. Every blueprint and every imagining behind this slice was Made in America.<br><br>I thought of General Tso’s chicken, which I have eaten exactly once in my life, at Panda Express.<br><br>Many foreigners love Chinese food. They especially love General Tso’s chicken.</p><h2 class=\"lm lm-sec\" id=\"en-lm6\"><span class=\"lm-no\">6.0</span></h2><p>Sacramento is dormant now, but it has awakened several times before. With every awakening, the city’s map is redrawn and several more holes are punched through its history.<br><br>Discourse is power. The Sacramento History Museum can speak: it tells of the cannery, of Mexican culture, of Native peoples. Sacramento’s reconstructed Chinatown can speak: it tells of cultural autonomy, of Chinese–Western synthesis, of ethnic harmony. But if we listen closely, we can hear other, smaller voices—from the cracks in the reinforced-concrete foundations, from the city map’s strange shape, from the holes in history.<br><br>I know history has its own rhythm. I also know that, in the face of structural changes in the economy and capital, some will inevitably profit and others lose; some will be uprooted and others rise. But forgetting and choosing to forget are not the same thing.<br><br>I remain angry, not because I want to curse the chains of cause and effect or the net of power. I simply refuse to let those who were dispersed, buried, and spoken for by others fall silent.<br><br>I remain angry because too many plans insist on “rebirth” but never on what comes before it: first, someone has to kill a Jesus.<br><br>I remain angry because there are two kinds of Genesis: either creation from nothing or creation by forgetting those fires and that scorched earth. The first happened only once. The second will happen countless times.<br><br>Do not forget those fires, that scorched earth. Do not forget those whom history has made absent. Do not forget the holes in the city.</p><section class=\"appendix\" aria-label=\"参考资料\"><h2 class=\"appendix-title\">参考资料</h2><ol class=\"appendix-list\"><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"en-ref-1\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">1</span><span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_tallest_buildings_in_Sacramento\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">List of tallest buildings in Sacramento</a>, Wikipedia, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"en-ref-2\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">2</span><span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UEUNt6_oYtI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PBS Documentary: REPLACING THE PAST - Sacramento’s Redevelopment History</a>, YouTube, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"en-ref-3\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">3</span><span><a href=\"https://themetropole.blog/2018/04/12/sacramento-city-of-redevelopments/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SACRAMENTO: CITY OF REDEVELOPMENTS</a>, The Metropole, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"en-ref-4\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">4</span><span><a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/zh-hans/%E7%AC%AC%E4%B8%80%E6%A2%9D%E6%A9%AB%E8%B2%AB%E5%A4%A7%E9%99%B8%E9%90%B5%E8%B7%AF#%E6%8B%9B%E8%81%98%E5%B7%A5%E4%BA%BA%E7%9A%84%E5%9B%B0%E5%A2%83\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">First Transcontinental Railroad</a>, Wikipedia, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"en-ref-5\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">5</span><span><a href=\"http://www.yeefow.com/past/1850.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">An Evidentiary Timeline on the History of Sacramento’s Chinatown</a>, Yee Fow, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></li></ol><p class=\"appendix-postscript\"><em>Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6.</em></p></section>","htmlZh":"<h2 class=\"lm lm-sec\" id=\"zh-lm1\"><span class=\"lm-no\">1.0</span></h2><p>在干燥的中央谷地的冲积平原上，萨克拉门托河和美国河相撞后合流南下，舒展成一个迤逦的Y字，加州首府就在这河流的东岸铺展开来。<br>可能是某个城市规划者患有自然恐惧症，由北至南的五号州际公路如堤坝一般紧贴河道而建，力劝该市居民对河流敬而远之。总之，从萨克拉门托中心城区向西眺望，双向八车道的人造河床代替了本该风景绮丽的水滨。而另一条东西走向的US-50高速公路如一根钢筋贯入这个城市的脊髓，将它生硬地拆分成南北两截。两条高速在城市的肚脐处交汇，形成一个巨大的X型创口贴。<br>为什么要贴两片？伤口太深的时候一个创口贴是遮不住的。</p><h2 class=\"lm lm-sec\" id=\"zh-lm2\"><span class=\"lm-no\">2.0</span></h2><p>走在萨克拉门托的市中心，我感觉回到了二十年前，那时候北京正在申奥，中学政治对世界形势的概括是“一超多强”。而那个超级大国彼时正风光无两。<br>萨克拉门托展现出的是这样一种古怪的现代化：它道路宽阔，街区齐整；楼宇林立，交通便捷。日光下，全玻璃的外墙雄辩地矗立。但这些大厦全都是二十年前修好的。以20年代的眼光看，它们虽然光鲜，但又确实落伍。<br>萨克拉门托州议会附近的所谓“机关群”（agency towers），几乎都是来自上世纪90年代开始的公共部门驱动投资的结果。<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#zh-ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\" data-n=\"1\" data-ref=\"zh-ref-1\"><span class=\"num\">1</span><span><a tabindex=\"-1\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_tallest_buildings_in_Sacramento\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">List of tallest buildings in Sacramento</a>, Wikipedia, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></span> 那时人们信心满满，以为这个城市的公共行业会无止境地膨胀下去，直到08年的次贷危机让一些野心家偃旗息鼓，于是天际线就固定成了如今这个样子。<br><br>走在萨克拉门托的市中心，我感觉这里是美国的东莞。<br>因为两年前的5月20（“我爱你”）日晚上，我在东莞的市中心曾经见到过两栋充斥视野的摩天大楼打出令人哑然失笑的标语：“热烈庆祝世界计量日”，以及“广告位等待招商”。那时我觉得东莞只是空有个体面的躯壳。经济转型和产业升级就像一座火山喷发，它们撒下的灰尘让东莞像庞贝一样凝结。<br>而萨克拉门托的火山灰则是从公共部门的收缩和政治叙事的枯竭两朵乌云中飘下来的。金融危机后，它赖以生存的规划、展望、远景、蓝图消失了，萨克拉门托的心气也就不复存在了。<br><br>走在萨克拉门托行人稀稀的市中心，我感觉这里是加州的雄安，但我没去过雄安，所以我希望我的感觉是错的。</p><h2 class=\"lm lm-sec\" id=\"zh-lm3\"><span class=\"lm-no\">3.0</span></h2><p>市区40英里外的美国河的上游是加州第一个发现金子的地方，所以萨克拉门托是加州淘金热的起点。那些冒险者从旧金山溯河而上来到这里聚集，这个城市就这样做为内陆港口生长起来，而它人来人往的老城区也就一直盘桓在河畔。<br>我用了两个词：老城区，盘桓。而这是一种不入流的说法。因为在50年代的官方的文件里它被描述成“一个令其颜面大失的贫民窟像污渍一样黏在了距离议会大厦只几步之遥的城市的面门上（one of the worst slum areas in the United States is on our own front doorstep adjacent to the seats of our city…）”。<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#zh-ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\" data-n=\"2\" data-ref=\"zh-ref-2\"><span class=\"num\">2</span><span><a tabindex=\"-1\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UEUNt6_oYtI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PBS Documentary: REPLACING THE PAST - Sacramento&#x27;s Redevelopment History</a>, YouTube, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></span><br>那时的美国正处在它的黄金年代：国库殷实，人民积极向上。虽然有一小撮吃得太饱的年轻人搞些让人耳聋的摇滚或鼓吹些虚无主义，但主流社会对此不以为意——进步、秩序、发展，这些才是正事。所以我们都知道接下来会发生什么。<br>二战后的萨克拉门托迎来了令它容颜换新的宏伟的再开发计划。<br><br>不知是否有意为之，开发项目中有一条依河而建纵贯南北的高速，刚好可以高效清理处在河滨的老城（要不是多事的肯尼迪总统出面干预，州际五号公路也不必特地拐个弯，保留下如今临河的一小块被当做标本的历史街区）。这旧城中的居民大多是少数族裔（华、日、墨、非）、薪资平平的工人阶级——是的，他们当然反对，但这毕竟不是主流民意。所以这些人无关宏旨地抗议了一下，最后也做鸟兽散了。在一段旧日的纪录片里，第一栋联邦大楼动土的那天，无数戴着西式礼帽的男女出现在废墟之上的奠基仪式中，他们手持铁铲，看上去很像一群体面的劳动人民。<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#zh-ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><br><br>我们曾接触过这样一个概念：举国体制。对于某些人，自上而下的庞大计划会过度干预市场，扭曲自然需求，容易失速、容易脱缰、容易形成危险的回音壁。<br>而在另一个语境内，有一个更精巧的说法：公共部门驱动的投资，也就是说，钱是公家和资本赚的，代价是弱势群体担的。萨克拉门托就是这个提法的先行者和典范。当年的再开发署创造出了日后风靡美国的税收增额融资制度（TIF: Tax Increment Financing，用未来增加的税收收入作为保障和偿还来源）吸引了大量投资，而这个再开发项目也获得了1963年美国的Ward Melville金质奖章。<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#zh-ref-3\" aria-label=\"Reference 3\">3</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\" data-n=\"3\" data-ref=\"zh-ref-3\"><span class=\"num\">3</span><span><a tabindex=\"-1\" href=\"https://themetropole.blog/2018/04/12/sacramento-city-of-redevelopments/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SACRAMENTO: CITY OF REDEVELOPMENTS</a>, The Metropole, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></span><br>在地价增长了75倍的州府大道下，萨克拉门托亲手斩断了自己的历史脉络，埋掉了它昔日文化多样的旧城。这不是一笔糟糕的主意。有些东西是不能细看的，让它们没入尘烟才皆大欢喜呢。</p><h2 class=\"lm lm-sec\" id=\"zh-lm4\"><span class=\"lm-no\">4.0</span></h2><p>当我走进萨克拉门托历史博物馆的时候，柜台处卖票的大娘冷不丁地问了一句：你们是知道咱这博物馆是YouTube上粉丝最多的博物馆才来的吧？我愣住了。<br>不是的大姐，我们就是来历史街区走走，但都到门口了也就进来随便逛逛。<br>后来我去它的账号上看过，号里面最热门的是一个老爷子操纵老式油墨机印制明信片的短视频。据那个大娘说，疫情的时候有个工作人员拍些博物馆的日常发着玩儿，然后就火了。她大概是不知道这玩意儿能不能火真是邪乎得很，反正这年头啥事儿都有机会出名五分钟的样子。<br>我这样说是因为这个博物馆完全不像世界第一一样引人入胜。它并没有什么拿得出手的展览。这栋二层小楼里分展三个主题：Campbell罐头加工厂的变迁，萨克拉门托的墨西哥文化的根脉，以及早年在这片平原上生活的印第安人。走马观花的话，半个小时也就够了。<br>真正的历史在这个博物馆外面。两百年前，这里是美洲大陆第一条横贯铁路的终点站。修通这条铁路1100公里西段线工程的工人中90%都是华工（上千人在这条工程中死去） <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#zh-ref-4\" aria-label=\"Reference 4\">4</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\" data-n=\"4\" data-ref=\"zh-ref-4\"><span class=\"num\">4</span><span><a tabindex=\"-1\" href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/zh-hans/%E7%AC%AC%E4%B8%80%E6%A2%9D%E6%A9%AB%E8%B2%AB%E5%A4%A7%E9%99%B8%E9%90%B5%E8%B7%AF#%E6%8B%9B%E8%81%98%E5%B7%A5%E4%BA%BA%E7%9A%84%E5%9B%B0%E5%A2%83\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">第一条横贯大陆铁路</a>, Wikipedia, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></span>；而这条终点站最终抵达的堤岸的正下方，正是萨克拉门托被埋葬的唐人街。<br>关于这一切，你在这个全世界粉丝数最多的历史博物馆里一个字也看不到。</p><h2 class=\"lm lm-sec\" id=\"zh-lm5\"><span class=\"lm-no\">5.0</span></h2><p>两百年前，中国的冒险者从旧金山登陆，来到萨克拉门托休整，然后去矿区碰运气。这些中国移民因此把旧金山称为“大埠”，而萨克拉门托则是“二埠”。巅峰时期，5000华人在此居住。<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#zh-ref-5\" aria-label=\"Reference 5\">5</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\" data-n=\"5\" data-ref=\"zh-ref-5\"><span class=\"num\">5</span><span><a tabindex=\"-1\" href=\"http://www.yeefow.com/past/1850.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">An Evidentiary Timeline on the History of Sacramento&#x27;s Chinatown</a>, Yee Fow, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></span><br>今天，萨克拉门托市中心仍然有一个名义上的“中国城”，这也是在60年代的再开发项目里规划好的。从沿河的高架下来开往市区的路上，你不可能错过它。这里松松散散立着七八栋房子，每一栋都戴一个中式的檐顶，与周围的气氛格格不入。如果你走进它，你可以看到一个写着“沙加缅度华埠”牌坊，这个坊上的朱漆有些褪色，但还是有些太新了。<br>从这里走进去可以看见一件叫“莲花”的泰国菜，除了旁边的老年公寓，这也是这里唯一一家开门的店。其他的建筑都大门紧锁，但看样子像是家族会馆或者祠堂一样的公共设施。整个街区阒无人迹，门可罗雀。<br><br>我并不为它的冷清感到寂寞。因为作为被西方和资本的审美人工授精的唐人街，它只是一个无根的偶像，一个徒具形式的纪念碑。它不代表任何东西，尤其不代表那些被拆毁了的生活、记忆、街坊、社区。<br>因为建造者想要给你看这样一个无伤大雅的历史切片，这个切片体现着权力者对华人群体的关心和补偿；这个切片上中华文化就是朱漆、屋檐、牌坊；这个切片影影幢幢，好像有很多人走过，但你叫不住其中任何一个人，也没有任何人对你的凭吊和叩问给出回答。因为这个切片用到的图纸和想象全部都是Made in America。<br><br>我想起在这辈子唯一一次在熊猫快餐吃到的左宗棠鸡。<br>很多老外都喜欢中国菜，尤其喜欢左宗棠鸡。</p><h2 class=\"lm lm-sec\" id=\"zh-lm6\"><span class=\"lm-no\">6.0</span></h2><p>萨克拉门托现在在休眠，但它曾经数度活跃过；而每活跃一次，这个城市的地图就要重新绘制，这个城市的历史就会被砸出几个窟窿。<br>话语即权力。萨克拉门托的历史博物馆会说话：它讲罐头厂、讲墨西哥、讲印第安；萨克拉门托重建的中国城会说话：它讲文化自治、讲中西合璧、讲民族和谐。但如果我们仔细听，我们还能听到另一些细小的声音，从那钢筋地基的缝隙里，从模样奇怪的城市地图里，从那历史的窟窿里。<br>我知道，历史有自己的节奏。我也知道，在经济和资本的结构性的变化面前，必然有人得利，有人失意；有人颠沛，有人腾达。但遗忘和选择遗忘终究不是一回事。<br><br>我保持愤怒，不是为了诅咒因果的锁链、权力的网缚，我只不愿那些被遣散被埋葬被代表的就此沉默。<br>我保持愤怒，是因为有太多计划都在强调“新生”，但不强调新生之前，首先要杀死一个耶稣。<br>我保持愤怒，是因为有两种创世纪：从无到有，或者忘记那些火和焦土。第一种创世纪只发生过一次，而第二种会发生无数次。<br>别忘记那些火，那些焦土。别忘记那些历史的缺席者，别忘记那些城市的窟窿。</p><section class=\"appendix\" aria-label=\"参考资料\"><h2 class=\"appendix-title\">参考资料</h2><ol class=\"appendix-list\"><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"zh-ref-1\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">1</span><span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_tallest_buildings_in_Sacramento\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">List of tallest buildings in Sacramento</a>, Wikipedia, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"zh-ref-2\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">2</span><span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UEUNt6_oYtI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PBS Documentary: REPLACING THE PAST - Sacramento&#x27;s Redevelopment History</a>, YouTube, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"zh-ref-3\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">3</span><span><a href=\"https://themetropole.blog/2018/04/12/sacramento-city-of-redevelopments/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SACRAMENTO: CITY OF REDEVELOPMENTS</a>, The Metropole, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"zh-ref-4\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">4</span><span><a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/zh-hans/%E7%AC%AC%E4%B8%80%E6%A2%9D%E6%A9%AB%E8%B2%AB%E5%A4%A7%E9%99%B8%E9%90%B5%E8%B7%AF#%E6%8B%9B%E8%81%98%E5%B7%A5%E4%BA%BA%E7%9A%84%E5%9B%B0%E5%A2%83\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">第一条横贯大陆铁路</a>, Wikipedia, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"zh-ref-5\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">5</span><span><a href=\"http://www.yeefow.com/past/1850.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">An Evidentiary Timeline on the History of Sacramento&#x27;s Chinatown</a>, Yee Fow, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></li></ol></section>","landmarksEn":{"kind":"sections","marks":[{"id":"en-lm1","kind":"sec","label":"1.0","title":"","peek":"On the alluvial plain of the arid Central Valley, the Sacrament…","minute":0},{"id":"en-lm2","kind":"sec","label":"2.0","title":"","peek":"Walking through downtown Sacramento, I felt I had gone back twe…","minute":0.8},{"id":"en-lm3","kind":"sec","label":"3.0","title":"","peek":"Forty miles outside the city, on the upper reaches of the Ameri…","minute":2.5},{"id":"en-lm4","kind":"sec","label":"4.0","title":"","peek":"When I walked into the Sacramento History Museum, the woman sel…","minute":5.3},{"id":"en-lm5","kind":"sec","label":"5.0","title":"","peek":"Two hundred years ago, Chinese adventurers landed in San Franci…","minute":6.8},{"id":"en-lm6","kind":"sec","label":"6.0","title":"","peek":"Sacramento is dormant now, but it has awakened several times be…","minute":8.6}]},"landmarksZh":{"kind":"sections","marks":[{"id":"zh-lm1","kind":"sec","label":"1.0","title":"","peek":"在干燥的中央谷地的冲积平原上，萨克拉门托河和美国河相撞后合流南下，舒展成一个迤逦的Y字，加州首府就在这河流的东岸铺展开来。","minute":0},{"id":"zh-lm2","kind":"sec","label":"2.0","title":"","peek":"走在萨克拉门托的市中心，我感觉回到了二十年前，那时候北京正在申奥，中学政治对世界形势的概括是“一超多强”。而那个超级大国彼时正…","minute":0.8},{"id":"zh-lm3","kind":"sec","label":"3.0","title":"","peek":"市区40英里外的美国河的上游是加州第一个发现金子的地方，所以萨克拉门托是加州淘金热的起点。那些冒险者从旧金山溯河而上来到这里聚…","minute":2.5},{"id":"zh-lm4","kind":"sec","label":"4.0","title":"","peek":"当我走进萨克拉门托历史博物馆的时候，柜台处卖票的大娘冷不丁地问了一句：你们是知道咱这博物馆是YouTube上粉丝最多的博物馆才…","minute":5.5},{"id":"zh-lm5","kind":"sec","label":"5.0","title":"","peek":"两百年前，中国的冒险者从旧金山登陆，来到萨克拉门托休整，然后去矿区碰运气。这些中国移民因此把旧金山称为“大埠”，而萨克拉门托则…","minute":6.9},{"id":"zh-lm6","kind":"sec","label":"6.0","title":"","peek":"萨克拉门托现在在休眠，但它曾经数度活跃过；而每活跃一次，这个城市的地图就要重新绘制，这个城市的历史就会被砸出几个窟窿。","minute":8.7}]}};
